(Discrepancy 2 - minimal repro. LANGUAGE.md:2116 says text to number "fails if
 text is not a valid number and sets the error flag"; :2027-2029 says a
 NON-default-base cast stops at the first invalid character and "does not set
 the error flag". Neither bullet describes what happens.
 One rule covers every base: parsing stops at the first invalid character, and
 the error flag is set only when ZERO characters parsed. So a base-10 partial
 parse does not fail, and a non-default-base total failure does set the flag -
 both bullets are wrong in opposite directions.
 The four casts below are the two bases crossed with the two outcomes.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox D2.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   12
   base 10 total failure: flag SET
   0
   18
   hex total failure: flag SET
   0
)
a text called partial is "12xy".
a number called 'base ten partial' is partial as a number.
On error Print "base 10 partial: flag SET".
Print 'base ten partial'.
a text called rubbish is "abc".
a number called 'base ten total' is rubbish as a number.
On error Print "base 10 total failure: flag SET".
Print 'base ten total'.
a text called mixed is "12g5".
a number called 'hex partial' is mixed as a hex number.
On error Print "hex partial: flag SET".
Print 'hex partial'.
a text called letters is "zzz".
a number called 'hex total' is letters as a hex number.
On error Print "hex total failure: flag SET".
Print 'hex total'.
